WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.930
In this video, you import a SUSE Virtualization cluster into Rancher. Once imported, you manage it from Virtualization Management and can provision Kubernetes clusters on it.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:18.030
In Rancher, open Virtualization Management and select Import Existing.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.390
Name the cluster as it should appear in Rancher.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.550
Add a description, for example the hardware it runs on.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.170
Select Create.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:34.970
Rancher shows the steps and a registration URL for this cluster. Copy the URL.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:43.060
Now open the SUSE Virtualization UI of the cluster you are importing, and go to Advanced, Settings.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:48.230
Find the cluster registration URL setting and open its menu.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.970
Select Edit Setting.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:59.050
Paste the registration URL. Leave TLS verification on, because Rancher has a trusted certificate.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.550
Select Save.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:11.670
A tip explains how to remove the cluster from Rancher later. Select OK, and the cluster starts connecting to Rancher.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:23.530
Back in Virtualization Management, the cluster is active and shows its SUSE Virtualization version and nodes. You can now manage it from Rancher.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:29.056
The cluster is imported. Next, you provision a Kubernetes guest cluster on it from Rancher.
